---
// The /iotm-tiers/ page: finally, our readers will know how much of a sicko i am.
// based on the google sheet; IOTM list comes from src/data/iotms.json (npm run sync-iotms).
import BaseLayout from "@layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import TierMark from "@components/TierMark.astro";
import TierCard from "@components/TierCard.astro";
import StandardMark from "@components/StandardMark.astro";
import IotmTable from "@components/IotmTable.astro";
import { IOTMS, SHEET_URL, STANDARD_SINCE, UPDATED } from "@lib/iotms";
---

<BaseLayout
  pageTitle="IOTM Tiers for Speedrunning"
  description="How much does every IOTM in KoL speed up a speedrun? We measure it, from the best at T0 to the worst at T6."
>
  <h1>IOTM Tiers for Speedrunning</h1>

  <p>
    Here at Loathers.net, our analysts spend a medically concerning amount of
    time analyzing KoL's myriad items-of-the-month and determining how many
    turns they save in a modern speedrun. Doctors HATE this one weird trick!
    This page is an attempt to help summarize what the heck our team is talking
    about when we rate the various IOTMs by their speedrun potential. This page
    pulls data from Captain Scotch's <a href={SHEET_URL}
      >IOTM tier spreadsheet</a
    >, which he has diligently maintained since early 2021.
  </p>

  <nav class="jump" aria-label="Jump to">
    <span
      ><a href="#every-iotm">Skip ahead to the full tier list</a>, or jump to a
      tier!</span
    >
    {[0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6].map((tier) => <TierMark tier={tier} />)}
  </nav>

  <p>
    IOTMs marked with <StandardMark /> are accessible in Standard speedruns (that
    is, released in {STANDARD_SINCE} or later).
  </p>

  <TierCard tier={0} saves="15+ turns">
    <p>
      The best-of-the-best. The cream of the crop. The big kahuna. These IOTMs
      save a boatload of turns by themselves, cutting over 15 turns every single
      day. These are the kinds of IOTMs that can shave entire days off your run
      if you only have a few shinies, and are still explicitly routed around and
      optimized if you have many. You probably need these!
    </p>
    <Fragment slot="example">
      <p>
        The <a href="/analysis/2025-10-blood-cubic-zirconia/"
          >Blood Cubic Zirconia</a
        > is a T0 IOTM. In a normal speedrun, you save 8-9 turns a day in flat savings
        from freekills. You can get the same or more turns saved from clever usage
        of refracted gaze. The ML effect helps in multiple areas of the run, likely
        saving you 1-2 turns per run. And you also get some bonkers buffs that could
        feasibly save turns, like +411% mysticality, all the spooky damage you need
        to ace the tower test, an extra 60% meat drop for nuns, and a huge boost of
        spell damage for advancing Blech House. And then on top of that you get a
        few turn-taking all-day banishes! That's WAY over 15 turns, man!
      </p>
    </Fragment>
  </TierCard>

  <TierCard tier={1} saves="7-15 turns">
    <p>
      These are great IOTMs. Some of them require synergies to operate
      successfully, but 7-15 turns is the kind of weighty margin that can
      separate a Bronze from a Silver in a challenge path leaderboard.
    </p>
    <Fragment slot="example">
      <p>
        The <a href="/analysis/2024-02-spring-shoes/">Spring Shoes</a> are a T1 IOTM.
        With 1 freerun every 30 turns, you can expect about 6-7 turns of value per
        day from freeruns alone. The +/- combat is worth 1-ish turn per day. Spring
        Kick is extremely difficult to benchmark, but is worth (in my view) at least
        2-3 turns per day by letting you powerfully manipulate the queue. The init
        and fruit are both worth about half a turn. So you're at about 11 turns saved
        per day. It's a very, very good IOTM.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
  </TierCard>

  <TierCard tier={2} saves="4-7 turns">
    <p>
      These are pretty good IOTMs. They don't individually make or break a run,
      and sometimes you can get away without having one or two of them, but
      there are a lot of them, and their savings add up.
    </p>
    <Fragment slot="example">
      <p>
        The <a href="/analysis/2025-03-leprecondo">Leprecondo</a> is a T2 IOTM. In
        modern Standard, you'll save a flat 5 turns per day from the spleen items.
        The familiar XP buff is worth a few turns per run in freed-up familiar flex,
        bar darts are probably worth a turn per run, and crafting plans are worth
        anywhere from 0 to 3 turns depending on the path and your crafting needs.
        There's perhaps a fraction of a turn or so from all the initiative buffs,
        as well. This nets out to an IOTM that saves you 6-7 or so turns per day in
        modern Standard; high-end T2, but T2 all the same.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
  </TierCard>

  <TierCard tier={3} saves="2-4 turns">
    <p>
      These are decent quality IOTMs. They will shave a number of turns in the
      aggregate, and are often necessary for big speedruns or useful in some
      synergistic ways with better IOTMs... but they probably aren't going to
      make your runs way better by themselves.
    </p>
    <Fragment slot="example">
      <p>
        The <a href="/analysis/2025-07-cooler-yeti/">Cooler Yeti</a> is a T3 IOTM.
        The turnbloat potential is helpful for cutting daycount for things like 1-day
        attempts or getting over the 3-day to 2-day jump, but the real value is in
        100 turns of +100% item drops and +100% meat drops; generally those kinds
        of effects aren't worth a ton by themselves, but 100 dang turns of it means
        you can fit the majority of your run's item drop activities (and nuns!) into
        the duration. In actual usage, my guess is that most runners will get the
        meat/item effect once a run and otherwise put this guy on the shelf. But that's
        totally fine, and still worth about 2 turns per day in the grand scheme of
        things.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
  </TierCard>

  <TierCard tier={4} saves="0-2 turns">
    <p>
      These are OK IOTMs. They have a few niche use cases, and some synergies,
      but it is certainly possible to get a Silver-quality leaderboard run
      without all of them. The savings are small, but they're dependable: a T4
      IOTM does at least a little something in every run.
    </p>
    <Fragment slot="example">
      <p>
        The <a href="/analysis/2026-01-eternity-codpiece">Eternity Codpiece</a> is
        a T4 IOTM. The simplistic “slot in a bunch of item drop, ascend, profit” method
        of using it probably saves you a turn or so over a full run. While that gets
        a bit more fractional as you lower the item drop percentage, it's still additive
        item drop compared to what you'd be getting naturally, and pulling a steely
        marble for +65 sleaze damage is likely to save you a turn. It also happily
        allays some equipment pressure by letting you double up on IOTMs in one accessory
        slot. I'm comfortable saying this saves anywhere from half a turn to a full
        turn per day; it does so dependably, too, which helps push this up from a
        T5 to a T4.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
  </TierCard>

  <TierCard tier={5} saves="Less than 1 turn">
    <p>
      These IOTMs aren't really necessary for speedrunning; they have small
      uses, but you should never bend over backwards to buy these or fit them
      into your plans. They can occasionally save a turn, but in many paths, or
      alongside IOTMs with similar functions, they save nothing at all.
    </p>
    <Fragment slot="example">
      <p>
        The <a href="/analysis/2026-07-portable-laughing-stock/"
          >Portable Laughing Stock</a
        > is a T5 KoL Con item. There's only so much you can do with nice buffs and
        a bit of turngen. These buffs are generally less useful than most buff-based
        IOTMs, too, essentially deriving their total value from the leveling assistance
        that the new fruit unlocks. There is some turngen benefit here as well, with
        4.5 adventures per fullness being on the high side (and a relatively easy
        path to 6-9 fullness per day taken up by the fruit)... but overall, this is
        a skippable item that often won't do anything for you.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
  </TierCard>

  <TierCard tier={6} saves="0 turns">
    <p>
      These IOTMs are virtually completely useless for speedrunning. Any turn
      savings you glean from them are fractional at best and non-dependable. In
      most cases, you're better off using literally anything else.
    </p>
    <Fragment slot="example">
      <p>
        The <a href="https://wiki.kingdomofloathing.com/Evolving_Organism"
          >unevolved organism</a
        > is a T6 KoL Con item. It's effectively useless. While it has a fun and cute
        mechanic (adding a new ability every 400 XP), none of the abilities it offers
        you are remotely useful for modern speedrunning. Familiar damage, HP/MP regen,
        and barrrnacle blocks haven't really been super useful in decades. Perhaps
        there'd be a run on these in 2008's low-MP meta, but for modern running...
        just don't use it. Trust me.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
  </TierCard>

  <h2 id="every-iotm">All IOTM Ratings (as of {UPDATED})</h2>

  <p>
    There are {IOTMS.length} items rated in the sheet, a collection of IOTMs, IOTYs,
    and KoL Con items. Use the filters above the table to narrow your scope to specific
    groups of IOTMs. Click a column header to sort by it, and hover over an IOTM function
    for a plaintext explanation of what it means. In the IOTM function column, solid
    tags perform that particular function directly, while dashed tags mean that the
    IOTM performs that function indirectly, either on a small marginal basis or through
    a specific synergy with another IOTM.
  </p>

  <IotmTable />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .jump {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 6px;
    margin: 10px 0;
  }
</style>
